The chat with host Jon Stewart opened with Ad-Rock recalling that he was not an original member of the trio that also included Adam &#8220;MCA&#8221; Yauch, who died in 2012 at age 47 following a fight with parotid cancer. &#8220;I was in a real band. We were called the Young and the Useless,&#8221; Ad-Rock (born Adam Horovitz) said of his teenage hardcore band.

When an early member of the Beastie Boys, guitarist John Berry, quit, the duo tapped their former opening act to join up. &#8220;They like to say I went from the minors to the majors,&#8221; Ad-Rock said of his glow-up into the group whose debut became the first rap album to top the Billboard 200 chart.

The old friends also reminisced about their favorite shows and venues from back in the day, with Ad-Rock, 60, shouting out the underground Manhattan club Pizza-a-Go-Go, and an early gig opening for horror punk legends the Misfits and the Detroit hardcore band the Necros. &#8220;We were the baby band on the punk rock bill,&#8221; Mike D (born Michael Diamond), 60, said of the gig where Ad-Rock was a spectator, but not yet a band member. &#8220;The Misfits were a real band, real records, outfits, you know, the hair, the whole thing. And we were like just kids carrying our own equipment.&#8221;

The free-wheeling conversation touched on the heady downtown music scene of the 1980s and how the gumbo of different sounds leaking out from the city&#8217;s clubs and scenes made the group realize they didn&#8217;t have to focus on one kind of music. &#8220;We don&#8217;t have to just make this one particular thing, why not create what inspires you?&#8221; Ad-Rock explained.

He also said, with all offense to Mike-D, that neither of them had the vision for what the band could be, a role that was left to their late comrade MCA. &#8220;I&#8217;m gonna lump Yauch in with us dumbasses here,&#8221; Mike replied a short time later. &#8220;In that time we were just hanging out by the bathroom at Irving Plaza, seeing this show or that show and talking about what was next week. And the idea that we were even going to be in the discussion of being one of those bands playing in one of those clubs was a reach beyond anything we would have foreseen.&#8221;

He recalled that when License to Ill dropped, the band thought it was a huge deal to play a show at The Ritz (now Webster Hall), as well as the Beasties taking a then-unknown Public Enemy on tour with them after hearing a demo of the group&#8217;s 1987 debut album, Yo! Bum Rush the Show .

The duo&#8217;s legendarily loose, loving relationship was on full display when Ad-Rock demurred &#8220;maybe I don&#8217;t want one,&#8221; after the conversation kept veering back to Mike&#8217;s recent debut solo album, Thank You . &#8220;You can want whatever you want, doesn&#8217;t mean I owe you!&#8221; Ad-Rock shot back at Stewart when he insisted on a second solo effort from the now-duo.

The limited-edition 40th anniversary edition of License to Ill is due out on March 18.

